  <p>Hartree–Fock shows why orbital language survives an interacting problem: a carefully chosen reference compresses much of the state into a manageable occupied subspace while retaining the mean Coulomb field and exact determinant exchange. Later theories either enlarge the wavefunction beyond one determinant or change the basic variable. Neither route makes every orbital eigenvalue an observable. A frozen-orbital removal estimate such as Koopmans’ theorem omits relaxation and correlation; charged and neutral excitations require their own physical objects.</p>
  <p>This is the conceptual bridge to the rest of electronic structure. The next theories will differ in what information they retain, what they approximate, and what they make computable—not merely in the software used to solve them.</p>

